WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.143
Validation logic should be tested like any other important application behavior.

00:00:05.143 --> 00:00:10.697
Unit tests should confirm that valid inputs pass and invalid inputs fail.

00:00:10.697 --> 00:00:16.251
Negative tests are especially important because validation exists to reject bad data.

00:00:16.251 --> 00:00:24.274
Test missing fields, extra fields, malformed values, unexpected types, type-confused inputs, excessive length, invalid formats, and business-rule violations.

00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:26.948
Boundary tests make contracts concrete.

00:00:26.948 --> 00:00:32.503
If a string allows one hundred characters, test one hundred and one.

00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:39.291
If a number must be between one and ten, test zero, one, ten, and eleven.

00:00:39.291 --> 00:00:47.314
If an array must contain a limited number of items, test empty arrays, maximum length, and over-limit input.

00:00:47.314 --> 00:00:52.868
These tests catch common mistakes caused by off-by-one assumptions and incomplete schemas.

00:00:52.868 --> 00:00:58.011
API contract tests can confirm that endpoints enforce the documented schema.

00:00:58.011 --> 00:01:04.388
They are useful when multiple clients, services, or teams depend on the same API.

00:01:04.388 --> 00:01:14.057
Fuzzing at a high level can help discover parser behavior and unexpected crashes, especially around complex JSON structures, encodings, and nested inputs.

00:01:14.057 --> 00:01:20.845
Fuzzing does not replace clear requirements, but it can find cases developers did not anticipate.

00:01:20.845 --> 00:01:23.931
Invalid input should be logged safely.

00:01:23.931 --> 00:01:34.011
Logging can help detect abuse and diagnose integration problems, but raw payloads may contain secrets, personal data, control characters, or intentionally noisy content.

00:01:34.011 --> 00:01:42.445
Test not only the happy path, but also how failures are reported, logged, rate-limited, and returned to the client.

00:01:42.445 --> 00:01:46.354
Predictable failure behavior is part of validation quality.
